  one of the chromosomes comes from our  mother and the other from our father and  the unique structure of chromosomes  keeps dna tightly wrapped around  spool-like proteins called histones  without such packaging dna molecules  would be too long to fit inside our  cells  so  when we think of this chromosome this  chromosome we can expand it and this is  what it looks like  so here's the actual dna this string  right here that is uh kind of  coiled around  uh these histones so if we continue and  unfold it you can see that  these are the histones and it's packaged  right around those histones  so  when we  speak about this code and what does this  code actually contain what instructions  does it contain so the product of the  code and the way that it interacts with  the environment is called a phenotyping  so a phenotype can describe a single  cell a tissue organ or the whole  organism phenotype of an organism might  include height size and activity and  phenotype of a cell might include its  specific function size and morphology or  its shape  so the code which is packaged in this  molecule defines this phenotype right  defines first phenotype of the cell then  of the organ or the tissue and then of  the whole organism right and so the the  basis of this code is all about these  nucleotides so these nucleotides are  made up of a sugar called deoxyribose in  nitrogen containing base and a phosphate  group the nucleotides are joined to one  another in a chain by equivalent bonds  that form between the sugar of one  nucleotide and the phosphate of the next  and the resulting  the the result of this structure is an  alternating sugar phosphate backbone so  each strand of dna molecule consists of  a sequence of bases that is  complementary to the base is an opposing  strain so when we say a base pair right  you will hear a lot when we speak about  genomics this term called base pair  nucleotide amino acid this is where it  comes from so a single  structure here is a base and the  connected  are base pair  this molecule can be read from both  sides you can see the three prime and  the five prime ends so you can read from  either side and this single base pair  represents some kind of a uh you know a  nucleotide so this nucleotide we would  refer to one of these adenine guanine  in cytosine  so to understand how this code works and  what is in this code as you all know  this is the famous starting point of the  human genome project you can see it was  published in the new york times on  tuesday june 27 2000 when they uh  announced that the this code of human  life is cracked by scientists right so  now scientists understand how these dna  sequences that we can now read  are organized to inform us about life  what is life how does life function how  does it produce the phenotype  now today we are in 2021  21 years later so if that happened 21  years ago what else do we need to learn  about this  so it turns out  that simply reading the code and  annotating it is a much longer process  than the human project itself the human  project itself was focused on  understanding what is in the code  and they discovered that there's only  one to two percent of protein coding  genes  the rest are non-protein-coding genes  and so we have a lot of other types of  information that is in the dna  and we now know that there is there are  a lot of questions there are a lot of  additional things to understand about  the human genome about the dna how it's  organized and how it actually works and  it's a very complex  approach that people now take to study  individual specific regions of the dna  and try to understand what is the impact  of how this code is written to describe  life and describe phenotype  so  as we spoke dna is stored in the nucleus  organized in chromosomes and specific  regions are responsible for specific  functions some of the easiest regions to  read and understand their function are  what we call protein coding genes  there's a total of  22  500 protein coding genes but even that  number continues to change  protein coding genes are the sequences  that are used to produce proteins and  then they also have a specific structure  so when we think of this code working or  producing that phenotype the way it is  done is by dna  being transcribed into rna and then  being translated into proteins  so these proteins  that are produced from dna is  facilitated by several different  proteins it's a complex kind of a  translational process  that uh you know we we will study uh in  a little detail to understand exactly  how it works but the basic concept that  we need to understand is that the  individual nucleotides or base pairs  that we saw in the dna eventually had to  have to be converted into amino acids  which is what the proteins are made up  of  and so when we think of protein coding  genes we're going to be looking at  sequences  dividing them up into triad codons  and then  looking at how those trinucleotide  codons sorry  and looking at what they mean how do  they function in terms of amino acids  so  the function of the rna polymerase is to  convert dna to rna and then the ribosome  of course converts the rna into protein  so the genome composition you know let's  just talk a little bit about how the  human genome is maybe different from  other genomes  the human genome is not the longest  genome you can see right here's the  human  grape for example is much longer not  much much longer but longer nevertheless  and uh influenza for example uh flu or  covet we all know is the thousands of  nucleotides long as opposed to the human  uh which really has uh  tens of thousands of genes uh and  billions of nucleotides so influenza for  example has eleven genes and uh you know  somewhere about twenty thousand uh  nucleotides uh and that's how many genes  we have so here you can see what types  of genes do we actually have and you can  see that nine percent so almost 10  percent of genes we don't even know what  they are but how do we know  that a gene is a gene so a gene is also  organized in a very specific way  they're not just sequences of  nucleotides they have upstream and  downstream regulatory sequences that  control transcription inside the gene  sequence itself there are codons and  introns that undergo splicing before a  gene is translated into a protein and  the length of these genes can vary  significantly and so they vary because  they have these introns that could be  very long  so when we speak about translation like  we said the translation occurs as a  result of ribosomes so here you can see  this is a microscope image of unfolded  dna you can see that the ribosomes are  these large dots right here and that is  what they look like so what happens is  mrna gets it into the dna it gets into  the ribosome and the outside of this  ribosome we already have this protein  which is a sequence that is folded  and eventually forms  a protein that looks like this  so this protein this is actually two  different proteins bound together  because proteins now have a function  they interact with each other and so in  the context of genomic data analysis we  can look at every step of the way we can  look at the dna we can look at the  impact of this dna on transcription and  we can look at the impact of dna in  terms of translation and so the ultimate  goal of genomics is to integrate  multiple  aspects of this kind of data by looking  at where the gene or genes are located  in terms of the chromosome  what do we know about the organization  of that gene and how that gene could be  translated into a protein so here you  can see that protein structure  and eventually to validate its function  through experiments so here you can see  this is the same protein this protein is  nm23h1 this is a protein that is  responsible for  control of metastasis or spreading in  cancer and you can see that its  structure is known because we have a  crystallized structure of 